.Dd August 21, 2026
.Dt SOWA-BOOTSTRAP 8
.Os Sowa
.Sh NAME
.Nm sowa-bootstrap
.Nd install Sowa into a filesystem someone else has mounted
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -from-tarball Ar tarball
.Op Fl -no-fstab
.Ar destination
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is
.Xr sowa-setup 8
with the disk half removed.
.Xr sowa-setup 8
owns the whole device - it partitions, formats, installs GRUB and writes a boot
menu - which means it can only produce its guided plain-partition layouts.
.Nm
takes a directory instead and copies the system into it, leaving the
partitioning, the filesystems and the boot loader to whoever ran it.
That is the way onto LVM, onto RAID, onto an encrypted root, into a custom
multiboot layout, or into a plain directory used as a chroot tree.
.Pp
.Ar destination
must already exist and be writable.
It does not have to be a mount point: a tree bootstrapped into an ordinary
directory is one of the things this program is for, and it says so rather than
refusing when the destination turns out not to be one.
A destination that is not empty is not refused either - installing beside an
existing system is another - but the confirmation says how many entries are
already there and that files with the same names will be overwritten.
.Pp
Without
.Fl -from-tarball
the source is the running system, which is only the right source when the
running system is Sowa; this is checked before anything is measured or copied,
so that a non-Sowa host is rejected at once rather than after several minutes
of walking its root filesystem.
.Pp
After the confirmation and before the copy,
.Nm
asks for the root password the installed system will have, twice, without
echoing it.
It is asked before the copy because the copy takes minutes and a question
waiting at the end of them is a question nobody is there for.
The image ships root with an empty password, which is right for a live system
and wrong for one being installed, so an empty answer is accepted and said out
loud rather than taken silently.
The question is skipped, and the account left as the image ships it, when
.Ev SOWA_BOOTSTRAP_ROOT_PASSWORD
already answers it, when
.Ev SOWA_BOOTSTRAP_ASSUME_YES
says the run is unattended, when standard input is not a terminal, or when
there is no
.Xr openssl 1
to hash the answer with - which on a foreign rescue system is a real
possibility, and the reason it is a skipped question rather than a fatal one.
.Pp
One part of the layout it does write:
.Pa /etc/fstab ,
generated from the filesystems mounted under
.Ar destination
and printed at the end for checking.
That part is not a guess - whatever belongs in the file is already mounted, so
it is read back with
.Xr findmnt 8
rather than chosen - which is why it is written and the boot loader is not.
See
.Sx THE GENERATED FSTAB .
.Pp
It ends by printing what is left to do - GRUB and a boot menu, with the fstab
above them to be read over - with the real device names filled in wherever they
can be established.
Both of those steps are deliberately printed rather than performed: the layout
is the caller's, and a boot loader written to a disk this program only guessed
at is the one mistake it must not make.
The instructions are given as
.Xr sowa-chroot 8
commands, so that the GRUB that runs is the installed system's own rather than
whatever the host has - a rescue environment shipping GRUB 0.97 is the case
that decided it.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "--from-tarball tarball"
.It Fl -from-tarball Ar tarball
Install from a rootfs tarball - one made by
.Ic make rootfs-tarball ,
or fetched from a mirror - instead of copying the running system.
The copy is then that image as it was built rather than the live root as it now
stands, and the host it is run from need not be Sowa at all.
This form requires
.Xr xz 1
as well as
.Xr tar 1 .
Everything else is the same.
.It Fl -no-fstab
Do not write
.Pa destination Ns /etc/fstab .
For the operator who has already written one, or who is bootstrapping a chroot
tree that will never boot.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print usage and exit.
.El
.Sh THE GENERATED FSTAB
Unless
.Fl -no-fstab
says otherwise, and unless
.Ar destination
is an ordinary directory rather than a mount point,
.Nm
writes
.Pa destination Ns /etc/fstab
from what
.Xr findmnt 8
reports mounted at and below
.Ar destination ,
overwriting whatever the copy put there - a tarball or a running installation
carries the fstab of the machine it was made on, and the image itself carries
none at all.
.Pp
Each filesystem is named by
.Cm UUID= ,
which fits every layout this program exists for: a logical volume, an
.Xr md 4
array and a plain partition all have one, where only the last has a PARTUUID.
It falls back to
.Cm PARTUUID=
and then to the device path, saying so when it does.
The kernel command line printed for
.Pa grub.cfg
still uses
.Cm root=PARTUUID= ,
because that is resolved by the kernel itself, with no initramfs and no
.Xr udev 7
to look a UUID up with.
.Pp
An ext2, ext3 or ext4 root filesystem gets pass 1, and another filesystem in
that family gets pass 2.
Filesystems without a traditional
.Xr fsck 8
pass, including XFS and Btrfs, get pass 0.
A
.Sy vfat
filesystem - the EFI System Partition, in every layout this is likely to meet -
gets
.Cm defaults , Ns Cm noauto ,
the same as
.Xr sowa-setup 8
writes, so that a machine whose ESP has been removed or reformatted still
reaches a login prompt.
Every entry is written
.Cm defaults :
the options a filesystem happens to be mounted with here belong to the
installing environment and not to the installed system.
.Pp
Two things are left out, both of them out loud.
Anything that is not a filesystem on a block device - the kernel's own
.Pa /proc ,
.Pa /sys
and
.Pa /dev ,
which
.Pa /etc/rc.d/rc.sysinit
mounts itself, along with tmpfs, overlay and bind mounts - is not an fstab line
here.
And swap is not a mount, so it cannot be read off the layout at all: the swap
areas
.Xr blkid 8
finds on the machine are written commented out, since one of them may belong to
another system sharing the disk.
.Pa /etc/rc.d/rc.sysinit
runs
.Ic swapon -a
after
.Ic mount -a ,
so uncommenting a line is all it takes.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
Interactive by default.
For unattended use:
.Bl -tag -width SOWA_BOOTSTRAP_ROOT_PASSWORD
.It Ev SOWA_BOOTSTRAP_ASSUME_YES
Set to 1 to skip the confirmation and the root password prompt with it.
.It Ev SOWA_BOOTSTRAP_ROOT_PASSWORD
The root password for the installed system, given instead of being asked for.
Unset, an interactive run asks; an unattended one installs the account as the
image ships it, with no password at all.
.It Ev SOWA_BOOTSTRAP_HOSTNAME
The hostname for the installed system.
.It Ev SOWA_BOOTSTRAP_QUIET
Set to 1 to leave out the closing instructions.
.It Ev SOWA_INSTALL_IGNORE_KERNEL
Set to 1 to install from a kernel older than 6.1 anyway.
See
.Sx CAVEATS ;
this is a way to watch it fail, not a way to make it work.
.It Ev SOWA_INSTALL_LIB
Where to find
.Pa install-functions .
The default is
.Pa /usr/lib/sowa .
This exists so that
.Pa sowa-install ,
the bundle that packs all three installers into one portable script, can run
from a directory of its own.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/usr/lib/sowa/install-functions" -compact
.It Pa /usr/lib/sowa/install-functions
the copy, the host-key reset, the mount points and the fstab, shared with
.Xr sowa-setup 8
and
.Xr sowa-chroot 8 .
.It Pa destination Ns /etc/fstab
written from the mounts under
.Ar destination ,
unless
.Fl -no-fstab .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
The three-command install onto a layout of your own:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
fdisk /dev/sda
mkfs.ext4 /dev/sda2
mount /dev/sda2 /mnt
sowa-bootstrap /mnt
sowa-chroot /mnt
.Ed
.Pp
From a rescue system that is not Sowa, using a rootfs tarball:
.Pp
.Dl sowa-bootstrap --from-tarball sowa-0.1-x86_64-rootfs.tar.xz /mnt
.Pp
A chroot tree in a plain directory:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mkdir /srv/sowa
sowa-bootstrap /srv/sowa
sowa-chroot /srv/sowa
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sowa-chroot 8 ,
.Xr sowa-pkg 8 ,
.Xr sowa-setup 8
.Sh CAVEATS
Must run as root.
.Pp
It also needs the kernel it runs on to be 6.1 or newer, which is not the kernel
the installed system will boot.
glibc here was configured
.Fl -enable-kernel=6.1.0 ,
so every binary in the image is entitled to assume every syscall that kernel
had.
On an older one the failure is quiet rather than clean:
.Xr statx 2
arrived in Linux 4.11 and coreutils 9 calls it directly, so
.Ic ls -l
answers
.Dq Function not implemented
and prints question marks where the permissions go, and
.Xr grub-mkconfig 8
writes a configuration with no menu entries in it.
The fix is a newer rescue system, not a switch.
